  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe prevailing pattern over the middle of the country for the next \nweek will be characterized by varying magnitudes/placement of \nupper-level ridging. Initially, that ridging is somewhat \nsuppressed with the ridge axis shifted east of the area. This is\nallowing a weak upper- level shortwave to spread cloud cover \nover the area as that wave tracks to the east through the \nregion. There have been some returns show up on regional \ncomposite reflectivity but with a deep layer of dry air through \nat least the lowest 12000 ft of the atmosphere, there is only a \nsmall chance for anything hitting the ground. A similar scenario\nwill play out tomorrow with the ridge suppressed to the \nsoutheast of the area. A weak upper-level shortwave trough will \nmove across the Central Plains, leading to mainly just \nincreasing cloud cover. With a slightly better moisture profile \nthough, can't rule out a brief shower or sprinkle, mainly over \neastern KS and adjacent areas of Missouri. Still, PoPs are \n10-15% or less through the area. \n\nBy the middle of the week, upper-level ridging strengthens through \nthe Plains states, leading to well above normal temperatures across \nthe area and suppressing any chance for precipitation. That persists \nthrough the end of the week before the ridge axis again shifts to \nthe east and then flattens out by early next week. As this occurs, \nit will allow for low chances for precipitation to return to the \narea. But even this looks confined to far northwestern Missouri and \nextreme northeastern Kansas. The rest of the area is still very \nlikely (>90%) to stay dry through the entire forecast.",
